          "bullets": [
            "Nvidia said on 23 November that its board had voted unanimously to reinstate Jensen Huang as President, Chief Executive Officer and a board director, effective immediately, after \"a series of discussions and meetings\" with him. The board said it \"received input from senior levels of the United States government\" and is confident the reinstatement \"poses no national security risk\". Shares rose as much as 36% during the session and closed 31% higher, per CNBC; The Straits Times puts the move at 31.5%, \"adding nearly US$1 trillion (S$1.29 trillion) to its market value\".",
            "Huang resigned on Nov. 13 after reports of extramarital affairs, including one with Alice Huang, a former Megaspeed executive whom US intelligence authorities suspect of working for Chinese intelligence; Beijing has denied the espionage allegations. CNBC reports the stock had shed nearly 33% from his 8 November admission, closing at $125.53 on Friday, against $186.64 on Nov. 6. CNBC says a petition for his return drew more than 200,000 signatures, according to its organisers.",
            "The reversal came less than a week after board chair Harvey Jones said on the 17 November earnings call that directors did not intend to reinstate Huang, and it moved the whole market: CNBC reports the Nasdaq Composite gained 6.5% and the S&P 500 4.7%, each its strongest session since April 2025. Colette Kress returns to being CFO only.",
            "The independent committee investigation, run with Wachtell, Lipton, Rosen & Katz, \"will proceed to conclusion\" and Nvidia says Huang is \"committed to cooperating with it\" — so the question of whether company secrets or national security were compromised is still open. Nvidia also says it is searching for a Chief Compliance Officer and has a new National Security and Export Control Oversight Committee, established 17 November."
