          ],
          "bullets": [
            "Anthropic's Economics team published a qualitative study on 23 November based on \"66 interviews with 58 of Anthropic's enterprise customers\", conducted face to face across 58 US companies with executives, managers and individual contributors. Nearly 300 AI applications were discussed in detail; of 100 pieces of AI-built software described in enough detail to categorise, 80% automated previously manual work and 20% replaced existing software.",
            "On employment: two-thirds of companies reported reduced hiring due to AI, about a third of those declines came from attrition without backfilling, and 10 companies reported hiring more because of AI. Two companies saw AI automation fully eliminate a position. Nine cut contracted developers, customer service, legal or back-office functions — one finance company downsized \"well over 100\" offshore data and developer contractors, and one company expected to cut reviewer hiring for the coming year by more than 90%. Five companies pushed out employees who would not adopt AI.",
            "The replacement cases are specific: a deal-tracker replacing a $10,000-per-year product, a legacy Windows XP application that would have cost $250,000 to replace, a 401(k) audit that previously took accountants 80 hours. Six AI-native startups estimated they would need about four times as many employees without AI. One COO said 90% of the operations budget went to AI tokens and 10% to headcount.",
            "Anthropic states plainly that the companies \"were not a random or systematically representative sample\", that value assessments were \"mostly qualitative, not from A/B tests\", and that an automated coder classified the applications. Every company interviewed is an Anthropic customer, so this measures AI's effect among firms that already bought it."
