    {
      "name": "Security, misuse & threat intelligence",
      "items": [
        {
          "headline": "OpenAI and Cisco release Shirudo, an eBPF runtime that blocks exploits on running Linux systems before a patch lands",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "OpenAI", "url": "https://openai.com/index/introducing-shirudo" },
            { "name": "The Register", "url": "https://www.theregister.com/security/2026/11/24/cisco-and-openai-pitch-ebpf-kernel-plugins-as-the-way-to-tame-the-patchpocalypse/5311795" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "OpenAI and Cisco published Shirudo on 23 November: an open-source runtime that uses eBPF to attach a temporary safeguard — a \"shield\" — to a running Linux service, so a vulnerable code path can be blocked without a restart. OpenAI says that in a controlled test \"a Codex-generated shield blocked a Java4Shell exploit attempt against a running Elasticsearch service without requiring a restart\", by adding an input check to Java's Base64 decoder and attaching it to the live process.",
            "Shields are cryptographically signed and verified before loading, checked by the Linux eBPF verifier, and recorded against a Trusted Platform Module so operators have \"a verifiable record of protections being loaded or removed\". The project is BSD 2-Clause licensed, with its BPF programs dual-licensed under BSD and GPL.",
            "The Register reports the motivation in numbers: Cisco and OpenAI \"think eBPF can help organizations cope with the flood of 100,000-plus software flaws reported this year, many after the debut of AI-powered bug finders\". That is the defensive half of a problem this briefing has covered from the offensive side all month — models finding bugs faster than operators can ship patches.",
            "OpenAI publishes no independent evaluation beyond the single controlled Java4Shell test, and Greg Brockman's framing — \"finding a bug today doesn't mean you can patch every system tomorrow\" — is a company statement, not a measured result. Shields still require an operator to approve each one."
          ],
          "topics": ["openai", "cyber-defense", "agents", "incidents"],
          "storylines": ["ai-enabled-hacking"],
          "impact": "beneficial",
          "flags": ["company-claim"]
        },
        {
          "headline": "Prosecutors say the arrested ex-OpenAI engineer used Codex to improve his hidden bug and sort the recordings",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "Rappler", "url": "https://www.rappler.com/technology/openai-engineer-abdulrahman-alfozan-vibecode-recording-devices-filing/" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "In a filing opposing bail, federal prosecutors said Abdulrahman Alfozan \"used OpenAI's Codex vibecoding application to improve his hidden recording device by adding voice-detection and using Codex to organize the recordings in Dropbox\". The Codex account was in his wife's name; she has not been accused of wrongdoing.",
            "Prosecutors laid out recordings spanning 15 folders dated September 8 to 28, covering meetings on 12 days in an executive conference room, including one file titled \"1200-sovereign-bilateral-briefing-sep22\". They said he photographed a confidential term sheet for a \"strategic partnership\" early on October 5, the same day a hidden camera recorded him checking behind the television where OpenAI's security team had found the planted device.",
            "This briefing covered Alfozan's arrest on 14 October; the new fact is the alleged use of OpenAI's own coding agent to build the device that recorded OpenAI. Prosecutors also described contact with Saudi officials, including a royal-family member who on October 6 sent a link to a Riyadh AI company and asked \"Time to move back to KSA and join us?\", to which Alfozan replied he might return \"after the IPO\".",
            "Prosecutors did not say the recordings were shared: \"What the defendant did or intended to do with those audio files remains under investigation.\" The filing did not identify any foreign government. Alfozan has pleaded not guilty; his lawyer called him \"an overly curious tinkerer\" and said most recordings \"apparently contain just random noise and silence\". He faces up to five years, a $250,000 fine and three years of supervised release if convicted at the January trial."
          ],
          "topics": ["openai", "threat-intel", "surveillance", "saudi-arabia"],
          "impact": "harmful",
          "flags": ["single-source", "update"]
        },
        {
          "headline": "Audit of five commercial AI agents finds post-deletion memory leakage in all of them, including health attributes",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "arXiv", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.29804" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "FORGETMENOT (arXiv:2611.29804, submitted 22 November) collected \"around 3800 multi-turn sessions comprising around 6M tokens across five popular AI agents\" and reports that \"user visible memory reveals substantially less (around 20% on average) attributes than the agents store in practice, exposing a significant transparency gap\".",
            "A \"stealth memory injection\" technique raised the amount of personal and sensitive attributes stored \"by 25% and 15% for Copilot and Claude, respectively\". Memory deletion was unreliable: \"all five agents exhibit non-zero post deletion leakage, including health and self-harm related attributes\". A proof-of-concept disclosure to a third-party email recipient gave Gemini a \"31.9% leakage rate\".",
            "Persistent memory is now default in consumer assistants, and the paper measures the gap between what a user can see and delete and what the system retains — the thing a privacy regulator would ask about.",
            "A preprint by Ismat Jarin, Tu Le and Athina Markopoulou; the arXiv abstract page lists no affiliations, and the vendors have not responded publicly. The paper does not name which agent each figure refers to beyond Copilot, Claude and Gemini."
          ],
          "topics": ["privacy", "agents", "agent-security", "microsoft", "anthropic", "google-deepmind"],
          "impact": "harmful",
          "flags": ["preprint", "single-source"]
        },
        {
          "headline": "Adaptive visual prompt injection beat web-agent defences 91.87% and 91.99% of the time across 21 configurations",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "arXiv", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.29268" },
            { "name": "arXiv", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.29977" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "\"Benchmarking Web Agent Defenses Against Adaptive Visual Prompt Injection\" (arXiv:2611.29268, submitted 22 November) built 360 webpage instances with 6 hijack strategies across 10 real websites and reports adaptive attacks averaging 91.87% success across 10 computer-use-agent defence configurations and 91.99% across 11 browser-use configurations.",
            "A second paper announced the next day, Conartist (arXiv:2611.29977, submitted 23 November), reports 32.12% attack success on a 576-instance tool-use benchmark and 66.67% on a Reddit GUI benchmark — improvements of 14.41 and 29.17 points over the prior best attack, across five backbone models.",
            "Together they say the same thing about deployed defences: the published numbers are measured against fixed attacks, and an attacker allowed to adapt gets most of the way through almost every published defence for agents that look at screens.",
            "Both are preprints with no institutional affiliation listed on their arXiv abstract pages, and both evaluate their own benchmarks. Neither tests the production configurations of named commercial agents."
          ],
          "topics": ["prompt-injection", "agent-security", "agents", "evals"],
          "storylines": ["agents-going-wrong"],
          "impact": "harmful",
          "flags": ["preprint", "single-source"]
        }
