    {
      "name": "Military, defense & geopolitics",
      "items": [
        {
          "headline": "Commerce adds seven Megaspeed-linked entities in Malaysia, Singapore and Thailand to the Entity List",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "Federal Register (BIS)", "url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/public-inspection/2026-23999/additions-to-the-entity-list" },
            { "name": "Semafor", "url": "https://www.semafor.com/article/11/23/2026/commerce-to-add-megaspeed-to-trade-blacklist" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "The Bureau of Industry and Security filed a final rule for public inspection at 21:15 UTC on 23 November adding 7 entities to the Entity List: MSFusion Sdn. Bhd., PT Megaspeed International and Speedmatrix Sdn Bhd in Malaysia; Apex Enterprise Solutions (Singapore) Private Limited, Megaspeed International Pte. Ltd. and Swiftdata Pte. Ltd. in Singapore; and Megaspeed (Thailand) Co., Ltd. in Thailand. The stated reason is \"the significant risk of these entities being or becoming involved in the diversion of advanced computing commodities to unauthorized end uses or end users\".",
            "The listings carry \"a license requirement for all items subject to the EAR\" and licences \"will be reviewed under a presumption of denial\". Shipments already en route may complete within 30 days of public inspection. The rule is scheduled for Federal Register publication on 2026-11-25 under docket 261123-0006.",
            "Megaspeed is the company at the centre of the Nvidia affair this briefing has tracked since the Alice Huang reporting, and the listing landed the same day Nvidia's board reinstated Jensen Huang. BIS spokesman Thomas Wackman told Semafor it is \"also now unlawful to export advanced SI chips to Megaspeed, even if the chips are produced outside the United States\".",
            "The rule itself does not name Nvidia, name China, or describe any specific diversion; the link to Nvidia chips reaching China comes from reporting and from the separate Justice Department investigation, not from this document. Semafor reports Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick had been under pressure from Treasury, the Pentagon, the State Department and USTR, who Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent said had backed the listing \"for months\" while \"Commerce holds the pen\"."
          ],
          "topics": ["export-controls", "chips", "nvidia", "china", "us-federal-policy"],
          "storylines": ["china-distillation-export-controls"],
          "impact": "neutral",
          "flags": []
        },
        {
          "headline": "Singapore's prime minister says the country will not be \"used and abused as a conduit\" around US chip controls",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "The Straits Times (via Yahoo News Singapore)", "url": "https://sg.news.yahoo.com/pore-working-us-chip-export-043621603.html" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "Speaking in Canberra on 24 November, hours after the Entity List filing, Prime Minister Lawrence Wong said: \"America has export controls on its chips. We do not enforce America's export controls, but we do not want Singapore to be used and abused as a conduit by companies to circumvent America's export controls.\" He said Singapore is \"working very closely with the US administration\" to \"work out a mechanism where America can do its own enforcement\".",
            "Wong said Singapore's own investigation is into whether Megaspeed breached Singapore law, that discussions with US authorities continue in order to establish the end users of the chips Megaspeed bought, and that Singapore is also looking into other companies that could be rerouting advanced AI chips. Asked whether the US had helped enough, he said: \"We did ask for some information. Not all of it was forthcoming.\"",
            "This is the first on-the-record account from the transshipment hub at the centre of the case, and it describes a gap rather than a partnership: Singapore says it lacks \"full information on the eventual destinations of these chips\".",
            "Wong said Singapore is not involved in investigations into Jensen Huang personally. The Straits Times report is the only account of these remarks this briefing could open; Reuters, Bloomberg and FT versions were unreachable."
          ],
          "topics": ["export-controls", "chips", "nvidia", "china", "singapore"],
          "storylines": ["china-distillation-export-controls"],
          "impact": "neutral",
          "flags": ["single-source"]
        }
