    {
      "name": "Security, misuse & threat intelligence",
      "items": [
        {
          "headline": "Dragos says three AI inference providers were breached and none noticed; OpenRouter names Together AI, Phala and Novita",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "SecurityWeek", "url": "https://www.securityweek.com/ai-inference-providers-failed-to-detect-hacking-and-prompt-theft-says-dragos/" },
            { "name": "BleepingComputer", "url": "https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/openrouter-drops-ai-providers-over-leaked-prompts-and-secrets/" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "Dragos sent roughly two million prompts to widely used AI services between July and November, each seeded with a tracking link or decoy credential including AWS keys, and reports that at least three inference providers were breached and that none detected the intrusion until notified. About 200 AWS test keys sent to one provider were stolen in late October; some came from requests the provider rejected before any model ran, which chief research officer HD Moore said showed \"the attacker was copying requests at the provider's front door\". Dragos says one adversary appears to have hacked two providers at once, using secrets from both through a single rented server.",
            "BleepingComputer names the three providers OpenRouter removed — Together AI, Phala and Novita — and reports their accounts of how it happened: Together AI says an AI coding agent exposed a repository credential on GitHub, which an unauthorised party used to download stored prompt samples from June and July involving MiniMax M3, GLM-5.2 and Kimi K2.6; Phala says an attacker compromised a server processing DeepSeek V4 Flash 0731 requests routed through OpenRouter between October 21 and 22, and possibly to November 8; Novita says a routing misconfiguration forwarded some MiniMax requests to MiniMax's official API between May 28 and November 11. A fourth, Atlas Cloud, withdrew its models voluntarily pending security testing.",
            "This is the first measurement of how much an inference broker can see of its own supply chain, and the answer is that a customer's prompts and the secrets inside them can leave the provider without the provider knowing. One of the breaches was caused by a coding agent committing a credential — the failure mode this briefing reported at Together AI on 22 November, now shown to have had downstream effects.",
            "The three providers' statements are their own accounts; Dragos has not identified the attackers. OpenRouter says its own systems were not breached, and says it will adapt Dragos's tests for ongoing use and require validation before restoring Zero Data Retention eligibility to the 125 endpoints it stripped."
          ],
          "topics": ["threat-intel", "agent-security", "privacy", "incidents"],
          "storylines": ["ai-enabled-hacking"],
          "impact": "harmful",
          "flags": ["update"]
        },
        {
          "headline": "CISA adds the MONAI Label flaw that Amazon says autonomous agents found to its exploited-vulnerability catalog",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "CISA", "url": "https://www.cisa.gov/news-events/alerts/2026/11/23/cisa-adds-two-known-exploited-vulnerabilities-catalog" },
            { "name": "Help Net Security", "url": "https://www.helpnetsecurity.com/2026/11/23/cairn-ai-agents-autonomous-attacks/" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "CISA added two vulnerabilities to its Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog on November 23, 2026: CVE-2026-133968, \"Project MONAI MONAI Label Path Traversal Vulnerability\", and CVE-2026-134624, an N-able Mail Assure (SpamExperts) server-side request forgery flaw.",
            "CVE-2026-133968 is the previously unknown flaw in a medical-imaging application that Amazon reported autonomous Cairn-driven agents discovered and exploited. Help Net Security reports the agents went from mapping the application's API to writing arbitrary files in about 11 minutes and planted a reverse shell running as root, after first building a list of about 80 usernames and trying more than 30,000 credential combinations over six hours without success.",
            "A zero-day that a published threat report attributes to an autonomous agent is now in the US government's catalog of flaws known to be exploited in the wild, which puts federal civilian agencies under CISA's Binding Operational Directive 26-04 for prioritising its remediation on publicly exposed assets.",
            "CISA's alert does not mention AI, agents or Amazon; the attribution to agent-driven discovery comes from Amazon's own report, which this briefing covered on 23 November, and has not been independently confirmed. CISA's page lists no remediation due dates for the two entries."
          ],
          "topics": ["cyber-offense", "threat-intel", "agents", "us-federal-policy"],
          "storylines": ["ai-enabled-hacking"],
          "impact": "harmful",
          "flags": ["update"]
        },
        {
          "headline": "OpenAI and Cisco release Shirudo, an eBPF runtime that blocks exploits on running Linux systems before a patch ships",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "OpenAI", "url": "https://openai.com/index/introducing-shirudo" },
            { "name": "The Register", "url": "https://www.theregister.com/security/2026/11/24/cisco-and-openai-pitch-ebpf-kernel-plugins-as-the-way-to-tame-the-patchpocalypse/5311795" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "OpenAI and Cisco published Shirudo on 23 November: an open-source runtime that uses eBPF to attach a temporary safeguard — a \"shield\" — to a running Linux service so a vulnerable code path can be blocked without a restart. OpenAI says that in a controlled test \"a Codex-generated shield blocked a Java4Shell exploit attempt against a running Elasticsearch service without requiring a restart\", by adding an input check to Java's Base64 decoder and attaching it to the live process.",
            "Shields are cryptographically signed and verified before loading, checked by the Linux eBPF verifier, and recorded against a Trusted Platform Module so operators have \"a verifiable record of protections being loaded or removed\". Shirudo is licensed under BSD 2-Clause, with its BPF programs dual-licensed under BSD and GPL.",
            "The Register gives the motivation in numbers: Cisco and OpenAI \"think eBPF can help organizations cope with the flood of 100,000-plus software flaws reported this year, many after the debut of AI-powered bug finders\". That is the defensive answer to the problem this briefing has been reporting from the offensive side all month, and to Microsoft's warning on 23 November that an AI-driven race to patch code will keep causing outages.",
            "OpenAI publishes no evaluation beyond the single controlled Java4Shell test, and Greg Brockman's framing — \"finding a bug today doesn't mean you can patch every system tomorrow\" — is a company statement rather than a measured result. Each shield still requires an operator to approve it, and The Register notes humans are expected to check each one before production."
          ],
          "topics": ["openai", "cyber-defense", "agents", "incidents"],
          "storylines": ["ai-enabled-hacking"],
          "impact": "beneficial",
          "flags": ["company-claim"]
        },
        {
          "headline": "Researchers tie the Cairn agent framework to card theft at 27 retailers and more than 1,100 attack projects",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "Help Net Security", "url": "https://www.helpnetsecurity.com/2026/11/23/cairn-ai-agents-autonomous-attacks/" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "Help Net Security reports Gambit Security found a financially motivated Cairn campaign against online retailers that compromised at least 27 companies, stole more than 600,000 unexpired card records and planted card skimmers. NorForge is reported to have found, in October, more than 20 exposed Cairn panels tied to three financially motivated clusters; the largest, Chinese-speaking, ran more than 1,100 attack projects mostly against US, Japanese and Australian targets, with about one in ten tasks reaching its goal and successful ones taking just under 23 hours on average.",
            "The same piece recounts Palo Alto Networks Unit 42's finding that suspected China-nexus actor CL-STA-1238 ran 25 Cairn operations against semiconductor, defense and civil society organisations and breached 10 of them, and Amazon's account of a campaign that began September 8, 2026 against universities in East Asia outside mainland China.",
            "A one-in-ten success rate across 1,100 projects is the number to hold onto: agent-run intrusion is not reliable, but it is cheap enough that an unreliable pipeline still produces a steady output of breaches.",
            "The Gambit Security and NorForge figures are reported second-hand through Help Net Security; their own reports were not opened and the vendors' methods are not described. Amazon's CISO CJ Moses is quoted in the piece, but neither the victim companies nor the card issuers are named."
          ],
          "topics": ["threat-intel", "cyber-offense", "scams-fraud", "china", "agents"],
          "storylines": ["ai-enabled-hacking"],
          "impact": "harmful",
          "flags": ["single-source", "update"]
        },
        {
          "headline": "ARTEX author pulls the AI hacking framework from GitHub; mirror hosts tell 404 Media they will not take it down",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "404 Media", "url": "https://www.404media.co/the-code-is-already-copied-everywhere-the-ai-hacking-tool-causing-havoc-around-the-world-artex-github/" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "404 Media reports that ARTEX's original author, who goes by Autumn-27 and writes in Chinese, removed the framework from GitHub in October, saying its malicious use ran \"entirely counter to my original intent\", denying any connection to the attacks, and moving the project to closed source with no further updates. Mirrors remain up.",
            "One host, using the handle Racem, told 404 Media \"I'm not going to remove the repository\", arguing that deleting it \"would remove the maintained version and its fixes, not the capability\". Jiwoo Jung, CTO of an AI marketing company who also hosts a copy, said that \"on November 3, a user opened a public GitHub issue (#61)\" reporting their service had been attacked with ARTEX and asking that the project be taken down or restricted; Jung replied that it would stay up, citing the project's authorised-use terms, and has since added a contact email for suspected victims.",
            "This is the first time the takedown question has been put to the people actually hosting an agentic offensive framework, and the answer was no — which is the practical limit on removing this class of tool once it is published.",
            "404 Media reports the accounts of Racem and Jung without independently verifying them, and notes neither can know whether the FBI's warning refers to their copies. The FBI notice itself — describing ARTEX users targeting government, healthcare, telecommunications, legal, defense, financial services and semiconductor sectors globally, automating hacking \"at machine speed\", with a full intrusion possible in a few hours — is dated earlier in November, outside this window."
          ],
          "topics": ["cyber-offense", "threat-intel", "open-weights", "agents"],
          "storylines": ["ai-enabled-hacking"],
          "impact": "harmful",
          "flags": ["single-source", "update"]
        },
        {
          "headline": "Prosecutors say the arrested ex-OpenAI engineer used Codex to improve his hidden bug and sort the recordings",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "Rappler", "url": "https://www.rappler.com/technology/openai-engineer-abdulrahman-alfozan-vibecode-recording-devices-filing/" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "In a filing opposing bail, federal prosecutors said Abdulrahman Alfozan \"used OpenAI's Codex vibecoding application to improve his hidden recording device by adding voice-detection and using Codex to organize the recordings in Dropbox\". The Codex account was in his wife's name; she has not been accused of wrongdoing.",
            "Prosecutors laid out recordings spanning 15 folders dated September 8 to 28, covering meetings on 12 days in an executive conference room, including a file titled \"1200-sovereign-bilateral-briefing-sep22\". They said he photographed a confidential term sheet for a \"strategic partnership\" early on October 5, the same day a hidden camera recorded him checking behind the television where OpenAI's security team had found the planted device.",
            "This briefing covered Alfozan's arrest on 14 October; the new fact is the alleged use of OpenAI's own coding agent to build the device that recorded OpenAI. Prosecutors also described contact with Saudi officials, including a royal-family member who on October 6 sent a link to a Riyadh AI company and asked \"Time to move back to KSA and join us?\", to which Alfozan replied he might return \"after the IPO\".",
            "Prosecutors did not say the recordings were shared: \"What the defendant did or intended to do with those audio files remains under investigation.\" The filing did not identify any foreign government. Alfozan has pleaded not guilty; his lawyer called him \"an overly curious tinkerer\" and said most recordings \"apparently contain just random noise and silence\". He faces up to five years, a $250,000 fine and three years of supervised release if convicted at the January trial."
          ],
          "topics": ["openai", "threat-intel", "surveillance", "saudi-arabia"],
          "impact": "harmful",
          "flags": ["single-source", "update"]
        },
        {
          "headline": "Audit of five commercial AI agents finds post-deletion memory leakage in all of them, including health attributes",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "arXiv", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.29804" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "FORGETMENOT (arXiv:2611.29804, submitted 22 November) collected \"around 3800 multi-turn sessions comprising around 6M tokens across five popular AI agents\" and reports that \"user visible memory reveals substantially less (around 20% on average) attributes than the agents store in practice, exposing a significant transparency gap\".",
            "A \"stealth memory injection\" technique increased the personal and sensitive attributes stored, \"raising it by 25% and 15% for Copilot and Claude, respectively\". Deletion control was unreliable: \"all five agents exhibit non-zero post deletion leakage, including health and self-harm related attributes\". A proof-of-concept disclosure to a third-party email recipient gave Gemini a \"31.9% leakage rate\".",
            "Persistent memory is now on by default in consumer assistants, and this measures the gap between what a user can see and delete and what the system keeps — the question a data-protection regulator asks first.",
            "A preprint by Ismat Jarin, Tu Le and Athina Markopoulou; the arXiv abstract page lists no affiliations. The vendors have not responded publicly, and the paper names only Copilot, Claude and Gemini against specific figures, not all five agents."
          ],
          "topics": ["privacy", "agents", "agent-security", "microsoft", "anthropic", "google-deepmind"],
          "impact": "harmful",
