{
  "headline": "Audit of five commercial AI agents finds post-deletion memory leakage in all of them, including health attributes",
  "sources": [
    { "name": "arXiv", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.29804" }
  ],
  "bullets": [
    "FORGETMENOT (arXiv:2611.29804, submitted 22 November) collected \"around 3800 multi-turn sessions comprising around 6M tokens across five popular AI agents\" and reports that \"user visible memory reveals substantially less (around 20% on average) attributes than the agents store in practice, exposing a significant transparency gap\".",
    "A \"stealth memory injection\" technique increased the personal and sensitive attributes stored, \"raising it by 25% and 15% for Copilot and Claude, respectively\". Deletion control was unreliable: \"all five agents exhibit non-zero post deletion leakage, including health and self-harm related attributes\". A proof-of-concept disclosure to a third-party email recipient gave Gemini a \"31.9% leakage rate\".",
    "Persistent memory is now on by default in consumer assistants, and this measures the gap between what a user can see and delete and what the system keeps — the question a data-protection regulator asks first.",
    "A preprint by Ismat Jarin, Tu Le and Athina Markopoulou; the arXiv abstract page lists no affiliations. The vendors have not responded publicly, and the paper names only Copilot, Claude and Gemini against specific figures, not all five agents."
  ],
